    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d51">

                <pb facs="0156" n="44" xml:id="pg44"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 835.73/83</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stimson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-10-28T13:00:00-04:16">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 28, 1918, 1 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>My April 9, 5 p.m. On October 23 the President finally signed a decree which
                    bears the date of August 1, authorizing the Central &amp; South American
                    Telegraph Co. to lay one or more cables between Argentina and Uruguay. The
                    decree was signed in accordance with the recommendations of both Attorneys
                    General of the nation, who declared the authorization to infringe upon no
                    existing concessions, and notwithstanding the bitter opposition of the Western
                    Telegraph Co.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
